A stream task may present the same abstract operation through very different input geometries: side-by-side panels, stacked panels, long stripes, or panels with unrelated color palettes. Do not infer a single fixed panel registration from one pair. For each demo, explicitly note panel boundaries, output dimensions, color roles (not literal color IDs), and which input positions correspond to special output cells. Compare the whole output mask to plausible predicates and mappings, then replay the candidate rule against every example before transfer.

In this instance, multiple intuitive panel-based submissions were wrong, and a later test grid was repeatedly guessed from isolated correspondences without establishing a rule that explained the full heterogeneous demo set. The practical lesson is to make cross-demo replay a hard gate, not to treat demos with different apparent geometry as irrelevant or to overfit the latest one. After any wrong submission, revisit the model and mapping before spending another attempt; avoid near-duplicate speculative grids. Reserve a final serialization audit for row widths, dimensions, and exact cell placement.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u074/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-panel-transfer-audit.md"}

